# Response headers for mechcrate.dev.
#
# `_headers` is NOT a Pages-only mechanism: wrangler uploads this file as part
# of the assets manifest for a Workers **static assets** deployment, and
# Cloudflare's asset worker applies it (locally, `wrangler dev` runs the same
# worker through workerd — see the "Edge headers" step in
# .github/workflows/site.yml, which proves the rules below against it).
# Syntax and limits are the Workers-assets ones, read out of wrangler 4.125.0
# (`workers-shared/utils/configuration/parseHeaders.ts` + `asset-worker`):
#
#   * a rule is a path line (must start with `/` or `https://`) followed by
#     indented `Name: value` lines; `# …` lines and blank lines are ignored;
#   * at most one `*` splat per path, 100 rules, 2000 chars per line;
#   * `! Header-Name` under a rule DETACHES that header from the response;
#   * a header set by two matching rules is comma-joined, not overwritten, so
#     no key below appears in more than one rule;
#   * rules match the REQUESTED URL pathname, not the asset that ends up being
#     served. That distinction matters for /404 — see the note at the bottom.

# Astro fingerprints everything under /_astro/ (content hash in the filename),
# so these are safe to cache forever. This is the one rule that actually moves
# a Core Web Vital.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Site-wide transport + privacy defaults.
# HSTS ships WITHOUT `preload`: preload is a one-way door (removal takes
# months) and it should not be requested until the http→https 301 has been
# live and healthy for a while. Adding `preload` is a deliberate later edit,
# not a default.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# The agent-facing artefacts. Cloudflare's asset server already derives
# text/plain from the `.txt` extension; re-asserting it here means a future
# change to that inference cannot quietly turn them into downloads. One hour
# of cache because they are regenerated on every deploy.
/llms.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/llms-full.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# Keeps the 404 page itself out of the index when it is requested DIRECTLY
# (crawlers do find /404 via stray links). It does NOT cover ordinary
# not-found responses: `not_found_handling: "404-page"` serves dist/404.html
# for an unmatched path, but the asset worker matches `_headers` rules against
# the requested pathname (`/typo`), never against the asset it resolved to, so
# no rule short of `/*` can reach those. `/*` would put noindex on the whole
# site, so the remaining coverage belongs on the 404 page itself as
# `<meta name="robots" content="noindex">` — a page change, tracked separately.
/404
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
